.TH RSHD 1 "" "" "mcos Manual"
.SH NAME
rshd \- the remote shell's daemon
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B rshd
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B rshd
takes
.BR rsh (1)'s
connections on TCP port 514, and answers rsh's search for hosts on UDP
port 514. It's a service:
.B rc rshd start
runs it, and
.B rc rshd enable
starts it at boot; it's off until then.
.PP
Each connection gets a session of its own (rshd runs itself with
.BR \-s ,
the connection as its input, as inetd ran BSD's rshd). A login runs
.B sh
on a pty as big as rsh's terminal: what rsh sends is typed into it, so
the pty does the terminal's work (echo, line editing, ^C), and what it
shows goes back. A command runs with
.BR "sh \-c" ,
on pipes: its input from rsh, its output and errors back apart, then how it
ended. When rsh goes, a login's line hangs up (SIGHUP to its shell), and a
command's input ends.
.SH FILES
.TP
.B /etc/rc.d/rshd
Its service.
.SH NOTES
No passwords and no encryption: anyone whose network card reaches this
computer's gets a shell on it, as with Berkeley's rshd before
.B .rhosts
files. Leave it off where that matters.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR rsh (1),
.BR rcp (1),
.BR rc (1)
